invariant: INV-16
file: p256_wide_mul.c
catches: p256_equiv_test
reason: table_select starts its scan at the row's second entry, so a
  digit of size 1 selects nothing and the multiplication adds the
  all-zero coordinates the scan started from in place of the entry. Every
  other digit is right, so one window in eight is wrong for a random
  scalar. bin/p256_equiv_test holds the base multiplication to
  p256_point_base_mul on scalars whose windows hold every digit, and
  proof/p256_wide_digit_harness.c proves the scan returns the entry at
  every index.

